<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tegea</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Tegea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Leading city of south-eastern Arcadia, long Sparta's rival and ally, famed for the temple of Athena Alea rebuilt by the sculptor Scopas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tegea formed from the union of nine villages in the high plain of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>. Its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-athena-alea">Athena Alea</a> was a regional cult centre by the 8th century BCE. In the 6th century BCE Tegea defeated a Spartan invasion in the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-fetters">Battle of the Fetters</a>', in which captured Spartans had to work Tegean fields in their own chains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> later prevailed, reportedly after recovering the bones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-mythology">Orestes</a> from Tegea, and Tegea became a leading member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>, fighting at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. Its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-athena-alea">Athena Alea</a>, burned in 395 BCE, was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scopas">Scopas</a> and was one of the finest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a>, Tegea joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mantinea">Mantinea</a> in founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arcadian-league">Arcadian League</a> in 370 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it passed among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>, coming under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tegea.jpg" alt="Tegea" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Gregor Hagedorn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_at_Tegea_(2017,_image_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tegea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Athena Alea</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arcadian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tegeia</dd></dl></aside></div>

